 The `notification` object supports message customization through the optional fields `customSms` and `customEmail` [1].

When `customSms` is provided, it must contain:
* `senderName` (string, required): the sender name displayed in the SMS, with a maximum of 11 characters [1];
* `text` (`CustomText`, required): the custom SMS text specified in Norwegian Bokmål (`nb`), Norwegian Nynorsk (`nn`), and English (`en`) [1].

When `customEmail` is provided, it must contain:
* `subject` (`CustomText`, required): the custom email subject in `nb`, `nn`, and `en` [1];
* `body` (`CustomText`, required): the custom email body text in `nb`, `nn`, and `en` [1].

The `CustomText` type requires the following fields:
* `nb` (string, required): text in Norwegian Bokmål [1];
* `nn` (string, required): text in Norwegian Nynorsk [1];
* `en` (string, required): text in English [1].

Additionally, if the `senderName` is (or in the future will be) protected by a third-party product such as SenderID, you must allow Altinn/Digitaliseringsdirektoratet as message producer [1].

Reminders may also override these texts: each object in the optional `reminders` list can include its own `customSms` and `customEmail` fields that replace the initial notification texts for that reminder; if omitted, the reminder inherits the texts from the initial notification [1].

 To register and inject a custom HTTP client such as `CountryClient` for use in `DataProcessingHandler`, follow the steps below. Note that these steps apply to **versions prior to v7** of the application template; for v7 and later, you should refer to the documentation on DataProcessing instead [1].

### 1. Register the client in the application startup
In `App/Program.cs` (.NET 6) or `App/Startup.cs` (.NET 5), add the required namespaces and register the typed HTTP client inside the `RegisterCustomAppServices` method [1]:

```csharp
using Altinn.App.client;
using Altinn.App.AppLogic.DataProcessing;

void RegisterCustomAppServices(IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration config, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    services.AddHttpClient<ICountryClient, CountryClient>();
    // Register your app's other custom service implementations here.
}
```

### 2. Inject the client into `DataProcessingHandler`
In `App/logic/DataProcessingHandler.cs`, add the namespace `Altinn.App.client` and make the client available by creating a constructor (the class does not have one by default) and storing the injected instance in a private field [1]:

```csharp
using Altinn.App.client;

public class DataProcessingHandler
{
    private readonly ICountryClient _countryClient;

    public DataProcessingHandler(ICountryClient countryClient)
    {
        _countryClient = countryClient;
    }
}
```

### 3. Update `App.cs` to provide the dependency
Because `DataProcessingHandler` is instantiated in `App.cs`, you must also inject `ICountryClient` into `App.cs` and pass it when creating the handler [1]:

- Add `using Altinn.App.client;` at the top of `App/logic/App.cs`.
- Add `ICountryClient` as the last parameter of the `App` constructor.
- Include it in the initialization of `DataProcessingHandler`:

```csharp
public App(
    IAppResources appResourcesService,
    ILogger<App> logger,
    IData dataService,
    IProcess processService,
    IPDF pdfService,
    IProfile profileService,
    IRegister registerService,
    IPrefill prefillService,
    IInstance instanceService,
    IOptions<GeneralSettings> settings,
    IText textService,
    IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor,
    ICountryClient countryClient) : base(...)
{
    // ...
    _dataProcessingHandler = new DataProcessingHandler(countryClient);
}
```

### 4. Use the client in logic
`_countryClient` is now available inside `DataProcessingHandler`, for example in the `ProcessDataWrite` method. Be aware that **stateless apps do not call `ProcessDataWrite`**; for stateless apps, use `ProcessDataRead` instead [1].

 To register and inject a custom HTTP client such as `CountryClient` for use in `DataProcessingHandler` in an Altinn app, follow the steps below. These steps apply to versions **prior to v7** of the application template [1].

### 1. Register the client in the application startup
In `App/Program.cs` (.NET 6) or `App/Startup.cs` (.NET 5), add the client registration inside the `RegisterCustomAppServices` method [1]:

```csharp
void RegisterCustomAppServices(IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration config, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    services.AddHttpClient<ICountryClient, CountryClient>();
    // Register your apps custom service implementations here.
}
```

Also add the required namespaces at the top of the file, including `using Altinn.App.client;` and `using Altinn.App.AppLogic.DataProcessing;` [1].

### 2. Inject the client into `DataProcessingHandler`
In `App/logic/DataProcessingHandler.cs` [1]:

- Add `using Altinn.App.client;`.
- Add a private readonly field for the client.
- Create a constructor that accepts `ICountryClient` and assigns it to the field (the class does not have a constructor by default).

```csharp
private readonly ICountryClient _countryClient;

public DataProcessingHandler(ICountryClient countryClient)
{
    _countryClient = countryClient;
}
```

### 3. Update `App.cs` to pass the dependency
Because `DataProcessingHandler` is instantiated in `App.cs`, failing to include the dependency there will cause a build error [1]. In `App/logic/App.cs`:

- Add `using Altinn.App.client;`.
- Inject `ICountryClient` as the last parameter of the `App` constructor [1].
- Pass the client when initializing `DataProcessingHandler`:

```csharp
_dataProcessingHandler = new DataProcessingHandler(countryClient);
```

### 4. Use the client in your logic
`_countryClient` is now available inside `DataProcessingHandler` and can be used in methods such as `ProcessDataWrite`. Note that **stateless apps do not call `ProcessDataWrite`**; for stateless apps you should use `ProcessDataRead` instead [1].

 To create the shipment message metadata for eFormidling in an Altinn app, you must implement the `IEFormidlingMetadata` interface and register that implementation in `Program.cs` [1][2].

**1. Implement the `IEFormidlingMetadata` interface**  
Create a class that implements `IEFormidlingMetadata` and build your shipment message inside the `GenerateEFormidlingMetadata` method [1]. This method must return a tuple of type `(string MetadataFilename, Stream Metadata)` containing the XML-serialized message [1].

A template implementation is shown below. You must replace `YourMessageType`, `yourMessage`, and the filename with your actual shipment message type [1]:

```cs
public class EFormidlingMetadata : IEFormidlingMetadata
{
  public async Task<(string MetadataFilename, Stream Metadata)> GenerateEFormidlingMetadata(Instance instance)
  {
      YourMessageType yourMessage = new YourMessageType();

      MemoryStream stream = new MemoryStream();
      XmlSerializer serializer = new XmlSerializer(typeof(YourMessageType));
      serializer.Serialize(stream, yourMessage);
      stream.Position = 0;
      StreamContent streamContent = new StreamContent(stream);
      streamContent.Headers.ContentType = MediaTypeHeaderValue.Parse("application/xml");

      return await Task.FromResult(("yourMessage.xml", stream));
  }
}
```

**2. Concrete example using `arkivmelding`**  
The source provides a concrete setup that uses the message type `arkivmelding` [1]. In that example, the `GenerateEFormidlingMetadata` method instantiates an `Arkivmelding` object, populates it with document descriptions (including the PDF and any attachments from the instance data), serializes it to XML, and returns `("arkivmelding.xml", stream)` [1].

**3. Register the class in `Program.cs`**  
Finally, register your metadata class by adding the following to the `RegisterCustomAppServices` method in `Program.cs` [2]:

```csharp
services.AddEFormidlingServices2<EFormidlingMetadata, EFormidlingReceivers>(config);
```

This ensures the application uses your implementation when the eFormidling workflow service task executes [2].

 For å legge til egendefinerte handlinger på en oppgave i prosessen til en Altinn-app, må du gjennomføre følgende steg:

**1. Legg til handlingene i prosessfilen (`process.bpmn`)**

Du må endre filen `App/config/process/process.bpmn` og legge til ønskede handlinger inne i oppgavens `<altinn:taskExtension>`-element, under `<altinn:actions>` [1]. Et eksempel på en oppgave med handlingene `demo` og `custom`:

```xml
<bpmn:task id="Task_1" name="Utfylling">
  <bpmn:extensionElements>
    <altinn:taskExtension>
      <altinn:taskType>data</altinn:taskType>
      <altinn:actions>
        <altinn:action>demo</altinn:action>
        <altinn:action type="processAction">custom</altinn:action>
      </altinn:actions>
    </altinn:taskExtension>
  </bpmn:extensionElements>
</bpmn:task>
```

Attributtet `type="processAction"` er standardverdien for typen, så også handlingen `demo` vil ha typen `processAction` [1].

**2. Definer nødvendige autorisasjonspolicyer (`policy.xml`)**

Brukerne må gis rettighet til å utføre de egendefinerte handlingene når de er i den aktuelle oppgaven. Dette defineres i `policy.xml` ved å opprette XACML-regler som matcher oppgaven, handlingen og brukerens rolle [1].

**3. Implementer tilpasset logikk i applikasjonskoden**

Den tilpassede koden assosiert med en prosesshandling utføres før prosessen flyttes til neste oppgave [2]. Opprett en ny C#-klasse som implementerer grensesnittet `Altinn.App.Core.Models.UserAction.IUserAction`. Grensesnittet krever at du definerer:

- En `Id` som samsvarer med navnet på handlingen i `process.bpmn` (f.eks. `"demo"`).
- En implementasjon av `public async Task<UserActionResult> HandleAction(UserActionContext context)` [2].

En enkel implementasjon kan se slik ut:

```csharp
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Altinn.App.Core.Features;
using Altinn.App.Core.Models.UserAction;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace Altinn.App.Actions;

public class MyDemoAction : IUserAction
{
    private readonly ILogger<MyDemoAction> _logger;

    public MyDemoAction(ILogger<MyDemoAction> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public string Id => "demo";

    public async Task<UserActionResult> HandleAction(UserActionContext context)
    {
        await Task.CompletedTask;
        _logger.LogInformation("UserId: {userId}, InstanceId: {instanceId}", context.UserId, context.Instance.Id);
        return UserActionResult.SuccessResult();
    }
}
```

Hvis `HandleAction` returnerer et `UserActionResult` med feltet `success` satt til `true`, flyttes prosessen til neste oppgave. Returneres det ikke en suksess, blir prosessen ikke flyttet, og API-et returnerer en feil til brukeren [2].
